**Recommendation: MAJOR REVISION.** I read v2 in full and cross-checked the relevant interfaces in c12rat, c12alg, uprime, and Drappeau’s Lemme 5. The predicted modulus/divisor/library-size obstruction does **not** materialize. The main remaining problems are an invalid marginal identity and an unjustified parent RAT weight bridge. Both appear repairable without changing the claimed exponents.

Repository files were kept read-only.

**Part A — repair audit**

| Issue | Verdict | Verification and remaining fix |
|---|---|---|
| **#1 OPV-A** | **Partly fixed** | [Steps 1–4](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/thmAB_results.md:414) correctly apply FO throughout and obtain the eighth moment by Hölder. The upper bound \(p_1\le12(d+1)c_1\) is independent and correct. The lower-bound argument has escaped the old dependency loop, but [step 6](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/thmAB_results.md:450) substitutes a block density for the actual marginal through an invalid identity. See below. |
| **#2 Parent normalization** | **Correctly fixed** | [W-P](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/thmAB_results.md:659) now uses a purely rational \(R_{\rm typ}\). P1’s exceptional same-\(\ell\) lines have the required rational normalization; P2 gives \(c_i\ll\rho_a(q)\); P3 uses an aggregate active-point lower bound. Thus the displayed normalized inequality follows with no missing \(\rho_a^{-2}\). |
| **#3 Root-conditioned subtraction** | **Correctly fixed** | [§11](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/thmAB_results.md:746) separates \(p=q\), includes both discriminant events for \(q'\ne q\), and treats \(q'=q\) separately. Use the **DW extension** of SQF, including averaging over \(w\), rather than fixed-\(w\) SQF(b) alone. The resulting \(D^C/B\) subtraction and endpoint condition remain valid. |
| **#4 Fibre covers/BTS** | **Partly fixed** | [Split first, then cover](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/thmAB_results.md:486) fixes the original short-piece problem. However, the printed verification establishes \(\gcd(a,\Delta')W\le2X_0\), whereas BTS requires \(\le X_0\). Specify the tighter placement below. The thinness cancellation at [line 518](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/thmAB_results.md:518) is correct. |
| **#5 Boundary Hölder** | **Correctly fixed** | [PD-A step 4](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/thmAB_results.md:562) uses the joint \((y,x)\) space and averaged moments. Keeping \(Z_y\), then comparing with \(Z_y^+\), is legitimate. |
| **#6 Marginal comparison/circularity** | **Partly fixed** | The independent upper bound breaks the OPV/EXC/PU loop. The lower comparison still needs the interior-cell argument given below; the equality currently printed is false. |
| **#7 Conditioned \(g\)-law** | **Partly fixed** | [§1.2](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/thmAB_results.md:108) correctly retains the zero-contribution component. “All ratios are unaffected” remains false for squared normalizations. The correction is \(1+O(1/q)\), with an additional \(O(1/q)\) rational variance term. |
| **#8 Cutoff** | **Correctly fixed** | [§1.3](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/thmAB_results.md:146) now imposes the two algebraic-density conditions actually needed. They imply the weaker near-zero estimate used in EXC-A, including the polynomial factors. |
| **#9 Wording/status** | **Partly fixed** | [§13](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/thmAB_results.md:875) correctly says “not quantified.” But [§0](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/thmAB_results.md:70) still says “not computable,” and [lines 45–46](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/thmAB_results.md:45) announce a completed further review while §15 marks it pending. |

For **W-P**, the three substantive checks pass:

- **P1:** \(R_{\rm typ}\asymp_{D^C}\widehat\rho_B(\mathfrak MV_1/Y)/\log Y\). If \(\ell\mid K\), first-point lines carrying that \(\ell\) cost \(D^C\log Y/\ell\), while their second-point density costs at most \(\Xi_pD^C R_{\rm typ}\log Y\). Hence the exceptional contribution is
  \[
  \ll\Xi_pD^C R_{\rm typ}^2(\log Y)^2/Y.
  \]
  There is at most one such \(\ell\), since \(K<Y^2\). Short corner lines must be discarded as in PUB before the linewise BTS application.
- **P2:** The filter and the \(b\)-window keep \(X_i(g)/(X_F/q)\) within a polynomial factor whose \(\gamma_2\)-power is bounded.
- **P3:** Active points provide both the rational one-point lower bound and the algebraic lower bound. For the latter, apply Hildebrand **after division by \(q\)**: the model intervals have relative length \(\gg1/d\) at scale \(X_F/q\). The weaker printed statement “relative length \(\ge1/(2dq)\)” alone would not verify Hildebrand’s interval hypothesis.

Consequently,
\[
\frac{E_e[R_1R_2c_1c_2]}{z_{\mathfrak q,e}^2}
\ll\frac{\Xi_pD^C}{n_p^2}.
\]

For **#3**, the complete subtraction ledger is:

\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
\text{exclusion}&\text{conditional upper bound}\\ \hline
p^2\mid F(P),\ p\ne q & p^{-2}+\text{negligible}\\
q^2\mid F(P)&O(q^{-1})\\
q'\mid F(P),\ q'\mid f_d\operatorname{disc}f,\ q'\ne q&O(d/q'^2)\\
q\mid f_d\operatorname{disc}f&O(d/q)\\
p\mid N,\ p\mid F(P)&\sum_{p\mid N}O(1/p)\ll\log N/B
\end{array}
\]

Also \(b>B_2\), so the parent’s \(b\)-exclusion contributes nothing. CRT endpoint errors remain negligible after summation. This gives \(D^C/B\); comparing with \(s_1s_2\) gives the stated \(a>2c_2\) condition at \(3/8\).

**Part B — explicit triage answers**

**T1 — YES, for the weighted transfer.**

CL correctly converts every gcd class, including full prime-power classes, into units modulo \(\mathfrak k=\Delta'/\delta\). For retained classes, \(\delta\le S_0<y\) eventually, so \(\delta\) is automatically \(y\)-smooth. Thus, on nonprincipal characters,
\[
\widehat a_i(\chi)
=\widehat u_{\xi_{\rm hi},\delta}(\chi)
-\widehat u_{\xi_{\rm lo},\delta}(\chi),
\]
using consistent half-open intervals. Characters themselves impose the coprimality condition.

The library scale is genuinely \(\xi/\delta\). The coefficients retain the actual factors
\[
(w_{\mathfrak q,e}/W)m_{\mathfrak q,e}^{-2}
E_e[\mathrm{Rat}_1\mathrm{Rat}_2\,\cdots],
\]
and their parent analogues. The proof bounds the \(g\)-integral and the label’s normalization; it does not require independence of the endpoints, geometry, or rational indicators.

For CF’s unrestricted statement, define normalized energies to be zero when \(A_i=0\).

**T2 — YES.**

Uniformly over the retained library,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{children: }&
\log Q=O(D^\alpha\lambda),&
\log(\xi/\delta)&\asymp D^{2\alpha}\lambda;\\
\text{parents: }&
\log Q=O(D^{1-\alpha}\lambda),&
\log(\xi/\delta)&\asymp D^{2-3\alpha}\lambda.
\end{aligned}
\]

The cutoff and division by \(\delta\) remove only \(o(\log x)\). Therefore \(Q\le(\xi/\delta)^{\eta_D}\) for sufficiently large \(N\), uniformly. Every nonprincipal character has conductor between \(2\) and \(Q\). Also \((\log x)^{c_D}\le y\le x\).

This matches the conductor-restricted, \(1/\varphi(k)\)-weighted statement of [Drappeau’s Lemme 5](https://drappeau.perso.math.cnrs.fr/documents/bffi-friable.pdf). Full BV/BDH is unnecessary.

**T3 — NO: there is no unpaid modulus, divisor-multiplicity, or library-size factor.**

Here is the quantitative ledger, with \(\bar\gamma=\max(\gamma_1,\gamma_2)\).

| Source | Children | Parents |
|---|---:|---:|
| Modulus weight times determinant multiplicity | \(\varphi(\mathfrak k)M(\mathfrak k\delta)/n^2\ll1/\delta\) | \(\ll d\log V_1/\delta\) |
| Mass of class \(\delta\) in blocks adjacent to an endpoint | \(D^C/\delta\) | \(D^C/\delta\) |
| Two cell densities relative to centers | \(e^{O(\lambda)}\delta^{2\bar\gamma}\) | Same |
| Rational-weight integral divided by squared mean | \(\Xi^C/n^2\), before multiplicity | \(\Xi_pD^C/n_p^2\), before multiplicity |
| Interval-to-endpoint energy normalization | \(\lambda^Ch_*^{-2}\) | \(\lambda^C(h_*^p)^{-2}\) |
| Endpoint grid count per label | \(D^Ch_*^{-1}\) | \(D^C(h_*^p)^{-1}\) |
| Sum over gcd classes | \(\sum_{\delta\ge1}\delta^{-2+2\bar\gamma}=O(1)\) | Same |

For fixed \((\delta,\mathfrak k)\), \(\Delta'=\delta\mathfrak k\) is determined. Hence no additional \(\tau(\Delta')\), determinant count, or number-of-divisors factor appears.

The **label-wide** child grid range has logarithmic length
\[
O\!\left(d\log(S_2T_0)+\eta_0\log N+C_X\log B\right),
\]
which is polynomial in \(D,\lambda\). The parent range has logarithmic length \(O(C_X\log B_2+\log d)\).

One must interchange the nonnegative sums label by label, as v2 now does. This pays the range for each label against its own weight; it introduces neither the number of labels nor a global maximum range.

Thus the final costs are
\[
\mathfrak D_c\le D^C\Xi^Ch_*^{-3},
\qquad
\mathfrak D_p\le D^C\Xi_p^C(h_*^p)^{-3}.
\]
The net bounds are
\[
\exp\{-cD^\alpha/\lambda^2+O(D^\theta\lambda^2)\},
\]
and
\[
\exp\{-cD^{3-7\alpha}/\lambda^2+O(D^{\theta_p}\lambda^2)\}.
\]
Their exponent gaps are
\[
\alpha-\theta=\tfrac12-\alpha>0,\qquad
(3-7\alpha)-\theta_p=2-5\alpha>0.
\]
Both gaps equal \(1/8\) at \(3/8\).

**T4 — YES, for the joint cell-moment estimate O1.**

FO on every retained block, LF-A’s centered second moment, the archimedean fourth moment, and Hölder using sixteenth moments provide a genuine proof mechanism. No independence assumption or slogan about covariance substitutes for these estimates.

There is an overstated intermediate drift bound, corrected below. The separate conversion from cell moments to \(p_i\ge c_i/2\) also needs repair. Neither defect reveals a first-order covariance term.

**Part C — ranked remaining and new issues**

**MAJOR — Invalid marginal identity.**  
At [OPV-A step 6](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/thmAB_results.md:450),
\[
\nu(x_1\text{ smooth},\text{ non-exceptional})
=c_1E[Y\tau(1+e)1_{\rm non-exc}]
\]
does not follow from a smooth count on each block. The marginal weights integers by their fibre lengths; the block density uses uniform coprime counting.

A finite counterexample to that identification is the half-open parallelogram
\[
(2,0)+[0,2)(1,0)+[0,2)(1,1).
\]
Its lattice points are \((2,0),(3,0),(3,1),(4,1)\). On the block \([2,4)\), the \(2\)-smooth density is \(1/2\). Multiplying by block mass gives \(3/8\), while the actual smooth mass there is \(1/4\).

**Fix:** Use interior cells and CF(ii):
\[
p_i\ge(1-\text{tiny})E_{\widehat\nu}\Psi_i
\ge c_i\{1-Ch-e^{-\lambda}-\sqrt{\mathcal V}-\text{tiny}\}
\ge c_i/2.
\]
This uses BL, EXC’s mass bound, and OPV’s moment estimate, without PU or boundary smoothness estimates. It closes the dependency cleanly.

**MAJOR — The parent RAT bridge is not supplied by value-law TV.**  
The child argument at [§13, lines 828–831](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/thmAB_results.md:828) is valid. Its “likewise for \(\sigma^{\rm rat}\)” is unjustified: parent acceptability depends on coefficients through \(f_d\operatorname{disc}f\), not only on \(F(P)/q\). Identical value laws can have different acceptability probabilities.

**Fix:** Specify RAT’s ideal parent weight precisely and prove the comparison using §11’s subtraction. The available absolute error is
\[
O(D^C/B+qN^{-\eta_0/2}),
\]
not merely the TV term. Relative to the parent smooth weight, it is at most
\[
\varepsilon_p
\ll \frac{D^C}{B\rho_a(q)}+N^{-\eta_0/3}.
\]
Then the elementary perturbation estimate gives
\[
\sigma_{\rm true}^{\rm rat}
\le(1+O(\varepsilon_p))\sigma_{\rm ideal}^{\rm rat}
+O(\varepsilon_p).
\]
This error is negligible in the open window and under \(a>2c_2\) at the endpoint. The repair is affordable, but must be incorporated into the stated RAT interface.

**MINOR — Overstated uniform \(\beta\)-drift.**  
At [line 426](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/thmAB_results.md:424), the displayed preceding bounds yield approximately
\[
O(D^{\zeta-2\alpha}/\lambda)
\quad\text{and}\quad
O(D^{4\alpha-2}/\lambda),
\]
for children and parents respectively, rather than \(O(D^{-2\alpha})\). At \(3/8\), these are \(D^{-7/16}/\lambda\) and \(D^{-1/2}/\lambda\), versus the claimed \(D^{-3/4}\).

**Fix:** Absorb these uniform errors into \(E_{\rm FO}\); their squares fit the stated variance budgets.

**MINOR — Complete BTS hypotheses.**  
At [lines 489–491](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/thmAB_results.md:489), choose the covering interval **inside the original positive dyadic range** \([t,2t]\), of length \(\max(|\text{piece}|,\lambda_{\min})\le t\). Then
\[
\gcd(a,\Delta')W\le |J|\le t\le\inf J.
\]
Moreover,
\[
\log W\ge\log X_2-C_X\log y-\log\vartheta-\log\Delta'
=(1-o(1))\log X_2,
\]
which verifies BTS’s second hypothesis too. Reflect negative pieces. Use the parent size bounds for parent fibres.

**MINOR — Zero-component normalization and status wording.**  
If the surviving \(g\)-component has mass \(t\), then
\[
\varphi_{\rm full}^{\rm alg}=\varphi_{\rm good}^{\rm alg}/t,\qquad
\varphi_{\rm full}^{\rm rat}
=\varphi_{\rm good}^{\rm rat}/t+(1-t)/t.
\]
These are harmless \(O(1/q)\) corrections, not exact cancellation. Also reconcile §0’s remaining “not computable” and completed-review claims with §§13 and 15.

The **(A1) removal passes**: only \(K=q\Delta\) is needed; \(q\)-power classes are handled without requiring \(\Delta<q\). The algebraic divided lattice also permits \(q\mid\Delta'\).

The **endpoint U′-DW re-check passes the listed exponent comparisons**: \(D^{5/8}\lambda\) absorbs the smoothness/TV costs, W-DW is unchanged, the L2 error ratios retain strict margins, and Kern-U’s height condition holds. I found no additional endpoint obstruction in §§12–14. This conclusion accepts the stipulated internal chain; the note itself correctly describes that re-check as less than a line-by-line proof.

